00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:34.020
everybody on this call,

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:36.190
will know that it's
never as easy as that.

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:42.060
So if you read some
GitHub ReadMes, and the ReadMe is

00:27:42.946 --> 00:27:47.800
two pages long and
requires some Linux dependencies, and

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:52.690
happens to works on
my machine syndrome,

00:27:52.690 --> 00:27:56.680
and as much as you can get
away from that, is best.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:28:00.715
We do that. I do that.
I try to do that by just

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:04.240
my particular solution
where I'm good at.

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:07.345
It's just to build R packages
around it. So we have

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:11.140
a range of R packages at
the bank which are specifically for

00:28:11.140 --> 00:28:13.060
data access. So
we have x-system.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:16.420
And this system has a way
of interacting with that

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:18.850
particular data. If it's
a database, or an API,

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:24.070
or something more bespoke,
just try and get it one line.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:26.192
I just want one command that

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:29.890
I can run, and it
gets the user from

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:34.330
zero to connected.
And as much of that

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:38.275
you can give to users, the better.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:39.930
Yeah,

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:44.560
never the easiest thing,
but as much as you can do,

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:45.445
you'll reap the benefits.

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:47.250
- Thank you.

00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:52.470
I see a big discussion
happening in the chat under one

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:55.200
of Mike's questions. So I'm
going to come back to that. But

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:59.220
there was a Slido question
that someone asked anonymously.

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:02.901
And it was, how does the
bank manage reproducibility

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:04.980
of analysis? I guess,

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:07.380
this kind of touches on
what you were just talking about,

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:10.500
but especially with the
thousand users with varying

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:11.770
levels of proficiency.

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:16.840
- Yeah, totally, so
I think it's, again,

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:19.770
some training goes into this.
I really think that Posit's

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:26.260
tools that actually do this
incredibly well. The reason that R

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:29.936
Markdown doesn't really have.
You can't just like embed.

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:34.480
You can't just like reproduce
tiny bits of R Markdown.

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:37.640
It's really meant to be
like start to finish,

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:41.240
which basically means this is
something I didn't realize till

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:43.450
after many years of using it.

00:29:43.450 --> 00:29:45.730
It's kind of like
reproducible by default.

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:49.210
And you actually have to
opt in to the non-reproducible bits.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:52.720
And the same thing with
your Posit Connect and some others.

00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:55.384
That element is

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:57.880
really good.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:00.280
But otherwise,

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:04.150
the usual sorts of things that
people on this call will know

00:30:04.150 --> 00:30:07.300
about, which is try and
make examples reproducible.

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:09.360
For example, in
the ref package.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:12.025
A lot about culture and just

00:30:12.910 --> 00:30:14.270
running things.

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:16.750
But also works in combination
with what I just said, which

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:20.020
is, it's easier if they
have easy data connections, right?

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:23.080
So if you're able to give
some people a data connection

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:25.840
package, then they can
do it, start to finish.

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:28.070
And I think a lot of
users are actually quite good at this.

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:32.320
A lot of users in my
experience are quite good at spotting, oh,

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:33.655
now I can run this app

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:36.370
because I've got
this data connection.

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:37.690
I'm not working with extracts.

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:43.600
And I think that the tooling
is a big part of that.

00:30:44.180 --> 00:30:44.680
- Thank you.

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:46.210
Mike,

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:49.100
for the question and discussion
that's happening in the chat.

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:53.090
Do you mind jumping in to
kind of summarize the point for me?

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:55.640
I was trying to multitask
and figure out what it was.

00:30:55.640 --> 00:30:56.830
And it was too hard.

00:30:56.830 --> 00:30:58.700
- Yeah, that's kind of
exploded. Sorry about that.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.450
So this is coming back
to James's point that

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:07.100
when you develop stuff, you
definitely don't want to own

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:08.525
that solution forever

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:10.460
or

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:14.000
maintain it because then that--

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:16.130
it's like if you
develop an R package

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:18.620
and you put it onto cran,

00:31:18.620 --> 00:31:21.725
you are forever the person
who's going to have to make

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:24.980
live with updating
that forever.

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:27.330
Similarly within

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:29.270
the business I'm in,

00:31:30.455 --> 00:31:31.400
we have a

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:35.120
kind of policy that I think
is similar to what James was

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:37.250
saying, where if
we're developing something,

00:31:37.250 --> 00:31:40.970
it's worth the time to take to
develop it in a way that the

00:31:40.970 --> 00:31:42.425
people we hand it off to

00:31:43.197 --> 00:31:45.600
can maintain it themselves.

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:49.730
Maybe not necessarily
expand on it greatly,

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.230
but if there are bits,

00:31:52.910 --> 00:31:56.000
they know where to tweak,
and they can get in there and

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:01.445
add a new variable or change
something from a to b quite readily.

00:32:03.350 --> 00:32:04.985
I'm trying to get away from--

00:32:06.020 --> 00:32:07.460
sometimes, what I see,

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:10.250
which is if someone comes
to you with a problem,

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:11.310
you solve that problem.

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:13.040
And you get it out the
door as fast as possible.

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:17.160
It is what it is. You just
deliver it and say, well, I'm done here.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:19.250
Typically, if you rush at it,

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:22.880
then it's harder for you and
for anyone else to maintain in

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:24.626
the long term. So

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:27.810
if you take a little bit
longer and give them the right

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:30.600
solution that's easy for
everyone to maintain going forward,

00:32:31.515 --> 00:32:35.100
it relieves the burden
on everyone. I think that's

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:37.390
what I can say.

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:41.540
- I can't help it, 100%
endorse all of that.

00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:44.440
I only have an example to
add which is like, I think

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:48.310
these can be straightforward
things like function names.

00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:52.285
So just take time to name
your functions. It's just

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:56.830
weird because it feels like
make work, but it's not

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:57.880
actually. It's

00:32:58.900 --> 00:32:59.400
just

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:04.180
naming functions, and naming variables,
and stuff. It's just a

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:08.140
really important part of
someone else understanding it

00:33:08.140 --> 00:33:10.457
in the future, and then
being able to audit it or

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:12.700
debug it in the future.

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:15.250
But yeah, 100% endorse.

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:16.750
- And sorry,

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:19.240
to kind of expand on
that, another example is

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:24.430
like markdown documents or reports
that you construct for someone,

00:33:24.430 --> 00:33:29.140
if you make them parameterized and what
you do is that you feed in--

00:33:29.140 --> 00:33:33.040
if you ingest like a YAML
file or something that drives that

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:36.980
parameterization, then they can always
expand it and add future

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:39.470
parameters and do all kinds
of clever things with it.

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:44.600
But it then means that you're
not locked into a given structure.

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:46.320
- Yeah, I understand.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:46.335
-

00:33:46.335 --> 00:33:50.450
I think we stay here for a few
more minutes on this topic given all the

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.870
comments there. But Donald,

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.270
I see you kind of asked
a question in that for,

00:33:56.990 --> 00:33:58.970
I guess, help and
convincing others.

00:33:58.970 --> 00:34:00.185
Do you want to jump in here?

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:04.730
Well, I can read it too
and feel free to jump in after.

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:06.620
But Donald had said
in that thread,

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:09.830
one of the biggest barriers
I face in building dashboards or

00:34:09.830 --> 00:34:13.610
packages is convincing people
that the work is worth the

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:17.570
maintenance costs from either my
team or theirs, and would

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:20.464
love additional discussions around this
or any thoughts you have.

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:22.565
- Yeah, so

00:34:24.260 --> 00:34:27.770
everything that I've kind of
said also applies to dashboards.

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:33.149
So dashboard design is just
also a hugely underrated skill and

00:34:33.949 --> 00:34:34.449
takes

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:37.250
time and experience
to appreciate

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:40.235
when a good dashboard
is made well.

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:49.959
I have the same quandary,
which is I try and say to

00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:53.860
managers or whomever I'm
trying to convince is,

00:34:56.830 --> 00:35:00.970
it's much more preferable
to have a smaller number of

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:05.410
insights conveyed well and
continuously than to have a

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:08.170
great wodge of insights to

00:35:09.970 --> 00:35:10.870
haphazardly

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:12.355
bandied together.

00:35:14.770 --> 00:35:16.750
The reason that's not always
easy is there's always somebody

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:18.175
who wants their favorite chart.

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:23.770
And that's where you kind
of get the dashboard fatigue phenomenon,

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:27.610
where everybody wants to put
their own like bit on the

00:35:27.610 --> 00:35:31.360
Christmas tree and eventually
the Christmas tree doesn't look

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:32.365
good or falls over.

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:35.270
And that's a hard thing.

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:38.830
And I think just manager
gumption to say that,

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:40.210
actually we don't do this.

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:42.850
We aren't going to
support like every chart.

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:48.170
We're not going to support
the broadest range of possible

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:52.325
visualizations for this thing. We
want to focus on core insights

00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:57.330
and things that the average
person is trying to learn or

00:35:57.330 --> 00:35:59.990
the average person is going
to want to know out of

00:36:02.210 --> 00:36:03.260
your output,

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:05.975
whether it's a dashboard
or whether that's something else.

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:07.220
- Thank you.

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:10.870
A question I had for you is,

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:13.510
thinking about all the
different teams that I've

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:16.420
spoken with, probably thousands
of teams across six years,

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:24.020
what is your infrastructure look
like in terms of testing

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:27.100
and rolling out new products?

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:30.840
I know some teams might
have one server, some have

00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:33.080
10 servers,

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:35.840
and they have their
own production and staging

00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:37.145
environment. And
I was just curious

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:41.490
with supporting so many users,
what that looked like for you?

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:41.960
- Yeah,

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:45.380
so this is a bit of a
journey that the bank has been on. So

00:36:45.380 --> 00:36:49.700
as we've sort of like grown
in a user base, we start to

00:36:49.700 --> 00:36:52.970
see this a lot more of that
when we do roll out a new

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:56.370
version of our own. I'm kind
of now the person responsible for

00:36:57.050 --> 00:37:01.400
testing it. But I can't test
the code of a thousand users.

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:05.810
For a start, like not everybody
is using version control, but we

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:07.550
are getting there.

00:37:07.550 --> 00:37:10.370
But even if they were all
using version control,

00:37:10.370 --> 00:37:12.930
we don't always have a
good catalog of that.

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:15.020
And that's part of
what I've tried to do,

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:17.550
it's to try and get
like a test bank.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:22.190
So these are things which
absolutely should work. So these are

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:24.282
unit tests and

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:27.320
regression testing
and integration tests.

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:30.140
And our

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:33.060
philosophy is it's a good idea.

00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:39.210
We try to get business in the
other areas of the bank to

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:40.260
contribute their code.

00:37:40.260 --> 00:37:42.795
That's not always easy
because they're busy as well.

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:44.300
And

00:37:45.750 --> 00:37:48.400
there's a couple of blockers
to being able to do that.

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:50.950
But I will also say
that it's, again,

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:53.175
something which just
takes time and energy.

00:37:53.175 --> 00:37:58.320
So I invest quite a lot of
time in putting together our test suites now.

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:03.820
And it's hard in the short term but
pays a lot of dividends in the long term.

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:06.510
The other thing which is
a bit useful for that is,

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:11.050
so my area of focus is kind
of on our data warehouse,

00:38:11.050 --> 00:38:13.650
so not everything in the bank
is based around its data warehouse,

00:38:14.250 --> 00:38:16.290
there are parts that are
outside that but the data

00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:17.310
warehouse is a good,

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:23.250
I want to say, forum for
that kind of testing because it is

00:38:23.250 --> 00:38:27.180
where a lot of things can end
up or should end up. And so

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:32.220
I'm also partly responsible
for the tooling on the data

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:35.115
warehouse. So that's my way
of bringing it together.

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:38.610
It might be similar
for those on this call.

00:38:39.360 --> 00:38:41.410
But just as much as possible,

00:38:41.410 --> 00:38:44.700
try and get people on board
to test it themselves or ask them

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:49.020
to contribute it or reach
out and ask their permission to

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:50.175
grab it yourself.

00:38:52.635 --> 00:38:54.000
Yeah, certainly
worthwhile doing.

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:55.290
- Thank you.

00:38:57.660 --> 00:38:57.980
I guess,

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:01.590
I just think a lot about
the teams who maybe started out

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:04.530
with just a few users and
are starting to grow and need to

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:08.625
expand that infrastructure
and how do they know

00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:12.240
what to do there, what
the next steps are.

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:17.880
I see lots of conversation
here, happening in the chat

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:19.350
here. I'm trying to
pull out the questions,

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:22.950
so feel free to just raise
your hand here if you have something

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:25.095
that wasn't really
answered yet.

00:39:27.510 --> 00:39:31.250
But there's a lot of
discussion happening around, like dashboard

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:35.620
design. I think once
we mentioned like dashboard fatigue.

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:40.920
And Eric, I see you had a
shout out to one of the conference

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:42.630
workshops, if you
want to jump in here.

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:44.160
- Yeah,

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:49.060
I was privileged to be a
TA at David Granjon and Maya Gans's

00:39:49.060 --> 00:39:53.230
workshop about effective design
principles for Shiny dashboards

00:39:53.230 --> 00:39:56.630
at PositConf and highly
recommend, checking that out,

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:59.500
as well as David's book
on outstanding shiny user

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:03.490
interfaces. And with the
tooling that now is in Shiny

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:05.200
itself in particular
with bslib,

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:08.470
it's becoming a lot easier
to incorporate some of these best

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:10.255
practices of design without

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:12.190
being an official

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:15.260
UX designer with
years of training.

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:18.310
It's something that I think a
lot of people are able to pick

00:40:18.310 --> 00:40:22.180
up on and hopefully avoid some
of the dashboard fatigue that can occur.

00:40:22.180 --> 00:40:24.710
- Yeah, I'll say something
a bit about this.

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:26.840
I am constantly struck by

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:29.080
just how similar--

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:30.360
well,

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:33.700
I mean the name does
it justice, right?

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:35.740
This is design and
design is hard, right?

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:39.490
So design takes place in
all facets of life, often

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:40.900
underappreciated,

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:44.660
easy to ignore even when it's
done right just like all designers.

00:40:45.410 --> 00:40:49.280
But I kind of feel of
the same way about dashboards that do

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:53.330
about good writing. So
good writing starts well, right?

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:56.540
The first sentence should
engage the reader in the same

00:40:56.540 --> 00:40:59.975
way that the first thing
that the dashboard shows.

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:05.030
The most important thing
or something not necessarily

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:09.080
gripping but something which you
would hope would introduce the

00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:14.660
rest of the concepts which
are to follow. Again, structure is

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:17.870
important. I mean, we're
very good, as humans well, I

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:21.890
guess because our education has
focused us on how to

00:41:21.890 --> 00:41:26.630
structure text very well
because we get lots of training

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:29.315
on this insofar as we have,

00:41:31.370 --> 00:41:32.540
structure headings,

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:35.345
citations, and
things like that.

00:41:36.240 --> 00:41:39.962
And we don't really get it
for dashboards, but a lot of the

00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:42.785
same things apply.

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:45.260
Notably, some exceptions too,

00:41:46.550 --> 00:41:48.365
but I see them as very similar,

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:51.305
especially asking yourself
the question, or sorry,

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:53.730
answering the question
on behalf of the user.

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:56.390
What are you trying
to communicate to them?

00:41:56.390 --> 00:41:59.490
What question do they have
